; Regression: a null TEST on a pointer-pointee out-cell stays refused, and the
; asymmetry with the STORE is the whole point (2026-08-28).
;
; ref_cell_null_store.scaly proves `set c: null` reaches the pointee of a
; `ref[ref[X]?]`. The mirror reading — that `c = null` must therefore be asking
; about the pointee too, since the reference itself is never null — is FALSE,
; and exempting the gate for it was tried and MEASURED WRONG:
;
;   * `set` REWRITES the target into the deref (set_target_stores_through#), so
;     the store genuinely lands in the pointee;
;   * `=` against null takes the NPO presence-test path and never loads, so the
;     comparison really is constant, exactly as the gate says.
;
; With the exemption in place `if c = null` answered FALSE for a cell holding
; null, and the unwrap two lines down trapped at exit 21 — a true diagnostic
; turned into a silent wrong answer, the one direction that must never happen.
;
; The honest spelling is the typed binding: `let v: ref[X]? c` and then
; `if v = null`, which ref_cell_null_store.scaly uses throughout.
;
; xfail: null test on a non-optional reference

define Item (v: int)

function peek(c: ref[ref[Item]?]) returns int
{
    ; the reference is never null; this comparison is constant
    if c = null
        return 0
    1
}

var itv Item(41)
var cell: ref[Item]? &itv
Console.println "`peek(&cell)`"
